{"reference":"2 Corinthians 11","book":"2 Corinthians","chapter":11,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Rivals and Credentials","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Paul’s descriptions of rival teachers are part of a heated defense. The “very chiefest apostles” language is ironic, and the people he attacks are not identified clearly enough to establish their ethnicity, motives, or precise teaching. The passage therefore calls for care in treating his accusations as polemic within a conflict, rather than as a neutral dossier on a recognizable group.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 11:4-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_011_6c2f10b2f6c226c6311632c3"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Paul acknowledges that he may be considered unskilled in speech, but insists that this does not mean he lacks knowledge. Elsewhere in the letter, rhetorical ability and personal presence are points of comparison; here he resists making polished delivery the measure of authority.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 11:5-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_011_831de746e8b18465258172cf"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Boasting Reversed","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"From verse 16 onward, Paul repeatedly labels his self-praise “foolish.” He nonetheless enters the comparison, matching claims about ancestry and service before turning the expected boast toward imprisonment, beatings, danger, deprivation, anxiety for the churches, and finally weakness. The catalogue functions as a counter-boast rather than a conventional display of honor.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 11:23-33","2 Corinthians 11:5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_011_0bf1c20409147457247d3fb5"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Suffering Is Not a Qualification Test","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The catalogue names violence, custody, dangerous travel, exhaustion, hunger, exposure, escape, and ongoing concern for communities. Its detail makes suffering central to Paul’s defense, but it does not turn injury or deprivation into a required proof of spiritual authority, nor does it make comparable trauma a test of loyalty or service.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 11:23-33"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_011_85742e972ad3d8e0c11c55a2"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The “Fool’s Speech” in Context","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"The boast continues beyond this chapter. Read across chapters 10–13, it appears to parody competitive self-promotion while also using its force: Paul meets rival claims with his own credentials, then emphasizes hardship and weakness rather than ordinary markers of honor. Because the opponents are known only through Paul’s argument, their number, origin, teaching, and relation to the Corinthian church remain uncertain.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 10:1-18","2 Corinthians 12:1-13","2 Corinthians 12:1-21","2 Corinthians 13:1-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_011_24c64cbd01e670a36068aa3d"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}